export const authenticationNextauthRbac = "## Introduction\n\n**Authentication is where most Next.js apps quietly leak — not because the login screen is broken, but because access control is trusted to the client.** Auth.js (formerly NextAuth.js) gives you a batteries-included primitive for sessions, OAuth, and credentials in the App Router, but it does not hand you a secure app for free. The hard parts — where the session lives, how roles flow into it, and *where* you enforce permissions — are decisions you own.\n\nThis guide walks through building authentication that holds up: choosing a session strategy, wiring OAuth and credentials, embedding roles via callbacks, and enforcing role-based access control (RBAC) on the server for every mutation. The through-line is one rule: **the client is a rendering hint, never a source of truth.**\n\n## Sessions vs JWT: the core strategy decision\n\nAuth.js supports two session strategies, set via `session.strategy`. This is the first and most consequential choice you make.\n\nWith the **JWT strategy** (the default), the session is a signed, encrypted token stored in an `httpOnly` cookie. There is no server lookup on each request — the token *is* the session. With the **database strategy**, the cookie holds only an opaque session ID, and every request reads the session row from your database.\n\n| Concern | JWT strategy | Database strategy |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Session storage | Encrypted cookie (JWE) | DB row + opaque cookie |\n| Read cost per request | Zero DB hit | One DB query |\n| Instant revocation | Hard (token valid until expiry) | Easy (delete the row) |\n| Horizontal scaling | Stateless, trivial | Needs shared DB |\n| Cookie size | Grows with claims | Tiny |\n| Best for | Serverless, edge, read-heavy | Admin tools, need force-logout |\n\n<div class=\"callout c-tip\"><span class=\"ic\">💡</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Pro tip</b><p>If you require the Credentials provider, Auth.js forces the JWT strategy — the database adapter's session model is bypassed for credential logins. Plan your revocation story accordingly, e.g. keep a short <code>maxAge</code> and a server-side deny-list of revoked <code>jti</code> values.</p></div></div>\n\nThe practical rule: default to **JWT** for its statelessness and edge compatibility, and reach for the **database strategy** when you genuinely need to invalidate sessions on demand (banning a user, \"log out all devices\", or immediate role downgrades).\n\n## Configuring Auth.js: providers and callbacks\n\nHere is a realistic `auth.ts` for the App Router combining an OAuth provider, a Credentials provider, and the callbacks that carry role and permission data.\n\n```ts\n// auth.ts\nimport NextAuth from \"next-auth\";\nimport GitHub from \"next-auth/providers/github\";\nimport Credentials from \"next-auth/providers/credentials\";\nimport { z } from \"zod\";\nimport { verifyPassword, getUserByEmail } from \"@/lib/users\";\n\nconst credentialsSchema = z.object({\n  email: z.string().email(),\n  password: z.string().min(8),\n});\n\nexport const { handlers, auth, signIn, signOut } = NextAuth({\n  session: { strategy: \"jwt\", maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 }, // 24h\n  providers: [\n    GitHub({\n      clientId: process.env.AUTH_GITHUB_ID!,\n      clientSecret: process.env.AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET!,\n    }),\n    Credentials({\n      credentials: { email: {}, password: {} },\n      authorize: async (raw) => {\n        const parsed = credentialsSchema.safeParse(raw);\n        if (!parsed.success) return null;\n        const user = await getUserByEmail(parsed.data.email);\n        // Constant-time compare inside verifyPassword to avoid timing leaks.\n        if (!user || !(await verifyPassword(parsed.data.password, user.hash))) {\n          return null; // Never reveal whether email or password was wrong.\n        }\n        return { id: user.id, email: user.email, role: user.role };\n      },\n    }),\n  ],\n  callbacks: {\n    // Runs on sign-in and whenever the JWT is read/refreshed.\n    async jwt({ token, user }) {\n      if (user) token.role = (user as { role?: string }).role ?? \"user\";\n      return token;\n    },\n    // Shapes the object your app sees via auth()/useSession().\n    async session({ session, token }) {\n      if (session.user) session.user.role = token.role as string;\n      return session;\n    },\n  },\n});\n```\n\nThe `jwt` callback is where you enrich the token *once*, on sign-in. The `session` callback then projects those claims into the session object your app reads. Do not query the database in the `session` callback on a JWT strategy — it runs on every session read and will silently become your hottest query.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Storing the entire user record in the JWT. The token lives in a cookie sent on every request — bloating it slows all traffic and can exceed the ~4KB cookie limit. Store only <code>id</code>, <code>role</code>, and a permission version; look up the rest server-side when needed.</p></div></div>\n\n## The OAuth authorization code flow\n\nUnderstanding what Auth.js does under the hood makes debugging redirect and callback errors far less mysterious. This is the standard OAuth 2.0 authorization code flow with PKCE.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant U as User Browser\n    participant A as Next.js App\n    participant P as OAuth Provider\n    U->>A: Click Sign in with GitHub\n    A->>U: Redirect with state and PKCE challenge\n    U->>P: Authorize request\n    P->>U: Redirect back with auth code\n    U->>A: Callback with code and state\n    A->>P: Exchange code for tokens\n    P->>A: Access token and profile\n    A->>U: Set httpOnly session cookie\n```\n\nThe `state` parameter defends against CSRF on the callback, and PKCE binds the code exchange to the client that started the flow. Auth.js handles both automatically — do not disable `checks` unless a non-compliant provider forces you to, and if you must, document why.\n\n## Putting roles and permissions in the token\n\nRBAC starts with a clean separation: **roles** are coarse labels attached to users; **permissions** are fine-grained capabilities that roles map to. Never scatter role-name string checks (`if (role === \"admin\")`) across your codebase — centralize the role-to-permission mapping.\n\n```ts\n// lib/rbac.ts\nexport const PERMISSIONS = {\n  admin: [\"post:create\", \"post:delete\", \"user:manage\"],\n  editor: [\"post:create\", \"post:delete\"],\n  user: [\"post:read\"],\n} as const;\n\nexport type Role = keyof typeof PERMISSIONS;\nexport type Permission = (typeof PERMISSIONS)[Role][number];\n\nexport function can(role: Role, permission: Permission): boolean {\n  return (PERMISSIONS[role] as readonly string[]).includes(permission);\n}\n```\n\nNow a permission check reads as `can(session.user.role, \"post:delete\")` everywhere. When permissions change, you edit one map. Because the role rides in the JWT, this check is a synchronous, zero-latency operation on the server.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-perf\"><span class=\"ic\">🚀</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Performance tip</b><p>Add a <code>permVersion</code> claim to the token. When you change a user's role, bump a server-side counter; if the token's version is stale, force a refresh in the <code>jwt</code> callback. This gives near-instant role changes without a DB read on every request.</p></div></div>\n\n## Protecting routes: middleware and server-side checks\n\nMiddleware is a coarse gate — it runs at the edge, redirects unauthenticated users, and keeps protected pages from rendering at all. It is **not** your authorization layer.\n\n```ts\n// middleware.ts\nimport { auth } from \"@/auth\";\nimport { NextResponse } from \"next/server\";\n\nexport default auth((req) => {\n  const isLoggedIn = !!req.auth;\n  const isOnDashboard = req.nextUrl.pathname.startsWith(\"/dashboard\");\n\n  if (isOnDashboard && !isLoggedIn) {\n    const url = new URL(\"/login\", req.nextUrl.origin);\n    url.searchParams.set(\"callbackUrl\", req.nextUrl.pathname);\n    return NextResponse.redirect(url);\n  }\n  return NextResponse.next();\n});\n\nexport const config = {\n  matcher: [\"/dashboard/:path*\", \"/admin/:path*\"],\n};\n```\n\nThe real enforcement lives next to the data. Every Server Action and Route Handler that mutates state must re-verify the session and the specific permission — because a request can hit an API route directly, bypassing any client-side UI or even middleware edge cases.\n\n```ts\n// app/actions/deletePost.ts\n\"use server\";\nimport { auth } from \"@/auth\";\nimport { can, type Role } from \"@/lib/rbac\";\nimport { db } from \"@/lib/db\";\n\nexport async function deletePost(postId: string) {\n  const session = await auth();\n  if (!session?.user) throw new Error(\"Unauthorized\");\n\n  if (!can(session.user.role as Role, \"post:delete\")) {\n    throw new Error(\"Forbidden\");\n  }\n\n  // Object-level check: does THIS user own or manage THIS post?\n  const post = await db.post.findUnique({ where: { id: postId } });\n  if (!post) throw new Error(\"Not found\");\n  if (post.authorId !== session.user.id && session.user.role !== \"admin\") {\n    throw new Error(\"Forbidden\");\n  }\n\n  await db.post.delete({ where: { id: postId } });\n}\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-warn\"><span class=\"ic\">⚠️</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Common mistake</b><p>Relying on middleware alone. Middleware can be skipped by misconfigured matchers, edge-runtime limits, and direct API calls. Treat it as UX (redirect to login), and put the authoritative check in the Server Action or Route Handler that touches the database.</p></div></div>\n\n## Object-level authorization: the vulnerability nobody tests\n\nRole checks answer \"can this *kind* of user do this action?\" They do not answer \"can *this* user act on *this* record?\" That gap is **Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA/IDOR)** — the most common critical API flaw. Note the ownership check in the example above: verifying the role is not enough; you must verify the relationship between the authenticated user and the target resource on every mutation.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Incoming mutation] --> B{Authenticated?}\n    B -->|No| R1[401 Unauthorized]\n    B -->|Yes| C{Has permission?}\n    C -->|No| R2[403 Forbidden]\n    C -->|Yes| D{Owns the resource?}\n    D -->|No| R3[403 Forbidden]\n    D -->|Yes| E[Execute mutation]\n```\n\n<div class=\"callout c-int\"><span class=\"ic\">🔥</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Interview question</b><p>\"A user changes the <code>id</code> in a DELETE request from their own post to someone else's — your role check passes because they are an editor. What is this and how do you prevent it?\" Answer: it is an IDOR / broken object-level authorization bug; prevent it with an ownership/tenant check scoped to the authenticated user on every query.</p></div></div>\n\n## Cookies, CSRF, and secret rotation\n\nAuth.js sets secure defaults, but you should understand and verify them. Session cookies are `httpOnly` (unreadable by JavaScript, blunting XSS token theft), `Secure` in production (HTTPS only), and `SameSite=Lax` (sent on top-level navigation but not cross-site POSTs, mitigating CSRF).\n\n- **CSRF:** Auth.js uses the double-submit-cookie pattern for its own routes and PKCE/`state` for OAuth. For your own mutations, prefer Server Actions (which are origin-checked) over hand-rolled state-changing GET requests.\n- **`AUTH_SECRET`:** required in production. It encrypts the JWT and signs CSRF tokens. Generate it with `openssl rand -base64 32` and store it only in your secrets manager.\n- **Secret rotation:** rotating `AUTH_SECRET` invalidates all existing sessions (tokens can no longer be decrypted). Rotate on a schedule and after any suspected compromise; communicate that users will be logged out.\n\n<div class=\"callout c-rec\"><span class=\"ic\">🎯</span><div class=\"body\"><b>Recruiter insight</b><p>Candidates who can articulate <i>why</i> the session cookie is <code>httpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code> — and what attack each setting stops — stand out immediately. Naming the settings is easy; explaining the threat model is what signals real experience.</p></div></div>\n\n### Session fixation\n\nSession fixation happens when an attacker forces a victim to use a session identifier the attacker already knows, then rides it after login. The defense is to **issue a fresh session on every privilege change** — Auth.js mints a new token on sign-in, so never reuse or accept a pre-authentication session identifier as authenticated.\n\n## Key takeaways\n\n<div class=\"takeaways\"><ul>\n<li>Choose JWT strategy for stateless/edge apps; use the database strategy when you need instant session revocation.</li>\n<li>Enrich the token once in the <code>jwt</code> callback and project it in the <code>session</code> callback — never query the DB on every session read.</li>\n<li>Map roles to permissions in one central module and check permissions, not role-name strings.</li>\n<li>Middleware is UX (redirects); the authoritative permission check belongs in every Server Action and Route Handler.</li>\n<li>Always add an object-level ownership check to prevent IDOR — role checks alone do not protect individual records.</li>\n<li>Rely on Auth.js secure-cookie and CSRF defaults, keep <code>AUTH_SECRET</code> in a secrets manager, and rotate it deliberately.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Should I use the JWT or database session strategy?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Use JWT for stateless, serverless, and edge deployments where you want zero database reads per request. Switch to the database strategy when you must revoke sessions immediately — for example bans, forced logout, or instant role downgrades. Note that using the Credentials provider forces the JWT strategy.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>Is middleware enough to protect my routes?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>No. Middleware is a coarse gate for redirecting unauthenticated users and improving UX. It can be bypassed by direct API calls and matcher misconfigurations. Always re-verify the session and the specific permission inside each Server Action or Route Handler that reads or mutates data.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>How do I add roles to the Auth.js session?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Attach the role to the token in the <code>jwt</code> callback on sign-in, then copy it into the session object in the <code>session</code> callback. Augment the Auth.js types via module declaration so <code>session.user.role</code> is typed. Keep only minimal claims (id, role, permission version) in the token to avoid cookie bloat.</p></div></details>\n\n<details class=\"faq\"><summary>What is the difference between authentication and authorization here?</summary><div class=\"ans\"><p>Authentication proves who the user is (handled by providers and the session cookie). Authorization decides what they may do (handled by your RBAC permission checks and object-level ownership checks). Auth.js primarily solves authentication; the authorization layer is yours to build and enforce on the server.</p></div></details>\n";
